Recommended Novel-Realism Addendum
Viewpoint integrity
Choose the viewpoint mode for each scene before writing:
close third-person;
distant third-person;
first-person;
controlled omniscient;
alternating limited viewpoints.
A viewpoint character can narrate only what they can physically perceive, remember, infer, misunderstand, or imagine. They cannot know another person’s exact thoughts unless the narrative mode permits it.
Every observation should have an observer.
The prose should distinguish:
what the character directly senses;
what they remember;
what they infer;
what they assume;
what they fear;
what is objectively true.
This prevents accidental omniscience and gives the reader a specific human consciousness through which to experience the world.
Viewpoint-distance control
The narrative should not remain equally close at all times.
Use:
Environmental distance when geography, approach, escape, weather, architecture, or social context matters.
Human distance when gesture, dialogue, posture, and interpersonal pressure matter.
Intimate physical distance only when contact, injury, hygiene, fear, bodily need, or close observation makes it relevant.
Move between these distances deliberately. Constant extreme closeness becomes exhausting and eventually less vivid.
