Perceptual filtering
A room should not be described as a neutral inventory.
Description passes through the current character’s needs.
A frightened person notices:
exits;
hands;
distances;
locks;
voices;
unstable surfaces;
changes in attention.
A tired person notices:
weight;
warmth;
pressure;
brightness;
repetitive noise;
the effort required to move.
A controlling person may notice:
hesitation;
compliance;
posture;
whether an instruction was followed;
signs that another person understands the power relationship.
The same room should therefore feel different through different minds without its objective geometry changing.
