Object biography
Important objects should have histories, limitations, and ordinary wear.
Instead of describing a generic chair, bag, towel, phone, or vehicle, establish:
who bought it;
why they chose it;
how old it is;
what condition it is in;
how it is normally used;
what small defect or habit its owner knows;
what practical limitations it imposes.
Do not invent elaborate backstories for every spoon. Give object history only when it affects recognition, handling, emotion, or plot.
A familiar object used in an unfamiliar way often produces more realism and psychological weight than an exotic custom prop.
