Character motor signatures
Give each character a stable but flexible movement identity.
Track tendencies such as:
stride length;
walking speed;
dominant hand;
how they sit;
whether they lead turns with the head or torso;
whether they occupy space or withdraw from it;
how they handle fragile objects;
whether they gesture while speaking;
how stress changes their movement;
what old injuries alter;
how fatigue exposes habits they normally conceal.
The same emotion should not produce the same posture in everyone. One person becomes still under anger; another moves faster. One fills silence with gestures; another reduces movement to avoid revealing themselves.
These signatures should evolve under circumstances without disappearing.
